  "live_records": {
    "q-162575": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162575",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\,i+xyz\\,j+2y\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y) \\\\  & =2x+xz+0 \\\\  & =2x+xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(2x+xz)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,2x+xz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
