    "q-162586": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162586",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x-z+2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=z\\,i-4y\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{3}) \\\\  & =0-4+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{z}^{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}(3{z}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x-z+2\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(3{z}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,3{z}^{2}-4\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-162586-a",
