    "q-162574": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162574",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ and the vector field $F=(yz-2x)\\,i+3y\\,j+(x+z)\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz-2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+z) \\\\  & =-2+3+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $3$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{3} \\\\  & =72\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
