    "q-162595": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162595",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=-1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y{z}^{2}\\,i-2yz\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+(-2z)+6z \\\\  & =4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{2}4z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =(2-(-1))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [2{z}^{2}]_{z=-1}^{z=2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot (8-2) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
